/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Chemistry and physics */
WB.L("atoms", "science", "beginner", "What atoms are made of",
  "Everything you can touch is made of atoms, and atoms are almost entirely empty space.",
  [
    ["Atoms are real", "The idea of atoms is ancient, but solid evidence came only in the last two centuries.",
      "The Greek thinker Democritus proposed around 400 BC that matter is made of tiny, uncuttable particles; atomos means 'uncuttable'. For two thousand years this stayed a guess, and Aristotle's view that matter is continuous won out.\n\nAround 1808, English chemist John Dalton revived the idea with evidence: elements combine in fixed proportions by weight, which made sense if they came in discrete units. Even then, many scientists treated atoms as a handy bookkeeping device. In 1905, Albert Einstein explained Brownian motion, the jittery dance of tiny particles in water, giving strong evidence that atoms really exist. Jean Perrin's experiments soon confirmed it."],
    ["Tiny, with a core", "Atoms are far too small to see with light, and each has a tiny dense nucleus of protons and neutrons.",
      "Atoms are roughly 0.1 to 0.5 nanometres across; a nanometre is a millionth of a millimetre. Hundreds of thousands of carbon atoms side by side would just span a human hair, and one grain of table salt holds around a billion billion atoms. Light microscopes cannot show them, because atoms are smaller than the wavelength of visible light. In 1989, IBM researchers spelled out their logo with 35 xenon atoms.\n\nJ.J. Thomson discovered the electron in 1897. Around 1909, Geiger and Marsden, working under New Zealand-born Ernest Rutherford, fired alpha particles at gold foil; a few bounced almost straight back. In 1911 Rutherford concluded that atoms have a tiny, dense, positive nucleus. James Chadwick found its neutral neutrons in 1932."],
    ["Mostly empty space", "Atoms are almost entirely empty space, yet matter feels solid because electrons repel each other.",
      "If an atom were the size of a football stadium, the nucleus would be about the size of a pea. More than 99.9% of an atom's mass sits in that nucleus, while nearly all its volume is space where electrons might be found. Quantum physics describes electrons as fuzzy clouds of probability, not tiny planets on neat orbits.\n\nSo why can't your hand pass through a table? Electrons repel other electrons, and a quantum rule called the Pauli exclusion principle stops them crowding into the same state. When you 'touch' something, your atoms never actually touch the object's atoms. What you feel is electromagnetic repulsion across a tiny gap."],
    ["Elements and isotopes", "Protons define the element; neutron counts create isotopes, and unstable ones decay at a steady rate.",
      "Every atom with one proton is hydrogen; six protons, carbon; eight, oxygen; 79, gold. That count, the atomic number, fixes the element. Atoms of one element can carry different numbers of neutrons. These versions, called isotopes, behave almost identically in chemistry but differ in mass and stability.\n\nCarbon-12 makes up about 99% of natural carbon. Carbon-14 is radioactive: its unstable nucleus breaks down, releasing alpha particles, beta particles or gamma rays. Each radioactive isotope has a half-life, the time for half a sample to decay. Carbon-14's is about 5,730 years, which allows radiocarbon dating of once-living material up to about 50,000 years old. Uranium-238's is about 4.5 billion years, which helps date rocks and the Earth. Many smoke alarms contain a trace of americium-241."],
    ["Electrons and quarks", "Outer electrons decide how atoms bond, and protons and neutrons are themselves made of quarks.",
      "Chemistry is mostly about electrons. Atoms are most stable with a full outer shell, so they share, give away or take electrons to form bonds. Sodium readily gives up its single outer electron and chlorine readily takes one, forming table salt. Carbon, with four outer electrons, shares them in long chains and rings, forming the backbone of every living thing. Helium's shell is already full, so it barely reacts, making it safe for party balloons.\n\nDeeper still, protons and neutrons are made of quarks, bound by the strong force. A proton has two 'up' quarks and one 'down'; a neutron has one up and two down. Electrons, as far as we know, are not made of anything smaller."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What finally turned atoms from a useful idea into accepted fact?", ["Ancient Greek arguments about uncuttable particles","Photographs taken with the first light microscopes","Evidence from chemistry and from jittery particles in water","Aristotle's reasoning that matter is continuous"], 2, "Dalton's chemistry and Einstein's explanation of Brownian motion gave real evidence where the Greeks had only a guess."],
    [1, "Why can't ordinary light microscopes show individual atoms?", ["Atoms are smaller than the wavelength of visible light","Atoms move too fast for any lens to focus on","Atoms are too widely spaced to appear in one view","Atoms are invisible in every kind of light"], 0, "Light cannot resolve anything smaller than its own wavelength, so other tools were needed."],
    [2, "If atoms are mostly empty space, why can't your hand pass through a table?", ["The nuclei of the two sets of atoms collide and bounce apart","The empty space in solids is filled with an invisible gas","Gravity locks the atoms of a table tightly together","Electrons repel other electrons, and a quantum rule stops them crowding together"], 3, "Solidity is the feel of electromagnetic repulsion across tiny gaps, not atoms touching."],
    [3, "What decides which element an atom belongs to?", ["Its number of neutrons","Its number of protons","Its number of electron shells","Its total mass alone"], 1, "The atomic number, the count of protons, fixes the element; different neutron counts just make isotopes."],
    [4, "Why does helium barely react with other atoms?", ["Its outer electron shell is already full","It has no electrons to share or take","Its nucleus is too heavy to take part in bonds","Its electrons are all in the nucleus"], 0, "Atoms bond to reach a full outer shell, and helium starts with one."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A table is mostly empty space. So why can't your hand pass through it?"});

WB.L("periodic-table", "science", "beginner", "The periodic table",
  "One chart organises every element in the universe, and when it was invented, it predicted elements nobody had found yet.",
  [
    ["A bold gamble", "Mendeleev's table left gaps for undiscovered elements, and their predicted properties proved right.",
      "By the 1860s, chemists knew about 60 elements but had no pattern linking them. Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev wrote each element's properties on cards and arranged them by atomic weight, grouping similar behaviour in columns. Legend says the answer came in a dream. His crucial move was boldness: he left gaps for undiscovered elements and described them, calling them eka-aluminium, eka-silicon and eka-boron.\n\nGallium (1875), scandium (1879) and germanium (1886) matched his predictions closely. When gallium's first density disagreed, he suggested the sample was impure, and a remeasurement proved him right. Today the table is ordered by atomic number, the count of protons, as Henry Moseley showed in 1913 using X-rays."],
    ["Rows and columns", "Each column, or group, holds elements with the same number of outer electrons, so they behave alike.",
      "Each row, or period, begins a new shell of electrons, and the table has seven periods. Each column, or group, holds elements with the same number of outer electrons, which is why they behave alike.\n\nGroup 1, the alkali metals such as sodium and potassium, are so reactive they are stored under oil and fizz or explode in water. Group 17, the halogens such as fluorine and chlorine, are highly reactive non-metals; chlorine compounds disinfect swimming pools. Group 18, the noble gases helium, neon and argon, barely react at all. Argon makes up almost 1% of the air you breathe."],
    ["Metals and the middle", "Most elements are metals, and the crowded middle holds the industrial workhorses.",
      "More than three-quarters of elements are metals: usually shiny, good conductors, and easy to hammer or draw into wire. Mercury is the only metal liquid at room temperature. Non-metals such as carbon, oxygen and the halogens cluster on the right. Between them, the metalloids silicon and germanium have mixed properties; silicon's controllable electrical behaviour underpins computer chips.\n\nThe wide central block holds the transition metals, including iron, copper, gold and platinum, many making coloured compounds and useful catalysts. The lanthanides, with scandium and yttrium, are the rare earths. Most aren't especially rare, just hard to find in concentrated, mineable deposits. Neodymium makes powerful magnets for wind turbines and electric cars, and Australia mines rare earths at Mount Weld in Western Australia."],
    ["Names and new elements", "Symbols often come from Latin names, and the heaviest of the 118 elements are made in labs.",
      "Some symbols reveal Latin names: Na for sodium (natrium), K for potassium (kalium), Fe for iron (ferrum), Au for gold (aurum), Pb for lead (plumbum, which gives us 'plumber'). The Swedish village of Ytterby lent its name to four elements. Curium, einsteinium and mendelevium honour scientists. IUPAC approves new names.\n\nAlmost all elements up to uranium (92) occur naturally; technetium and promethium are found only in tiny traces. Heavier ones are made by smashing atoms together. Element 118, oganesson, completed the seventh row, named in 2016 with nihonium, moscovium and tennessine. Superheavy atoms last milliseconds or less. Physicists suspect an 'island of stability' where some might last longer, and labs are chasing elements 119 and 120."],
    ["Made in stars", "Light elements came from the Big Bang; heavier ones were forged in stars and stellar explosions.",
      "Hydrogen, helium and a little lithium formed in the first few minutes after the Big Bang, about 13.8 billion years ago. Stars then fuse lighter elements into heavier ones, building carbon, oxygen and more up to iron, where fusion stops releasing energy.\n\nElements heavier than iron form mainly in extreme events, such as supernova explosions and collisions between neutron stars. A neutron star merger observed in 2017 was seen producing heavy elements, probably including gold and platinum. The calcium in your bones and the iron in your blood were made inside stars that died before the Sun was born. As Carl Sagan put it, we are made of star stuff."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Mendeleev's table convince chemists it reflected something real?", ["It listed every element in the order they were discovered","It predicted unknown elements, and later discoveries matched closely","It was the first table to include more than 100 elements","It placed every element at exactly its measured weight"], 1, "Accurate predictions of gallium, scandium and germanium showed the pattern was real."],
    [1, "Why do elements in the same group behave alike?", ["They have the same number of outer electrons","They have the same atomic weight","They were discovered around the same time","They have the same number of neutrons"], 0, "Outer electrons drive chemical behaviour, so each column shares similar reactions."],
    [2, "Why are rare earth elements hard to supply, despite most not being especially rare?", ["They break down quickly once they are dug out of the ground","They are found only in a handful of laboratories","They are mostly used up by making magnets","They rarely occur in concentrated deposits that are easy to mine"], 3, "Scarcity here is about finding mineable concentrations, not about total abundance."],
    [3, "Why are the heaviest elements known only in tiny amounts?", ["They exist only on other planets, out of our reach","They have been banned from being produced in labs","They are made a few atoms at a time and decay within fractions of a second","They react instantly with air and vanish"], 2, "Superheavy nuclei are unstable and last milliseconds or less before decaying."],
    [4, "Where did the iron in your blood come from?", ["It formed in the first minutes after the Big Bang","It was forged inside stars that died before the Sun formed","It was made inside the Sun","It was created when Earth cooled"], 1, "Fusion in stars builds elements up to iron, and dying stars scattered them into space."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A chemist described three unknown elements before anyone had found them."});

WB.L("chemical-reactions", "science", "beginner", "Chemical reactions",
  "Cooking, rusting, burning, breathing. Chemical reactions rearrange atoms into new substances, and they're happening in you right now.",
  [
    ["Atoms rearranged", "In a reaction, atoms rearrange into new substances, and none are created or destroyed.",
      "In a chemical reaction, bonds break and new bonds form, producing substances with different properties. Hydrogen and oxygen are gases, yet burning hydrogen in oxygen makes water. In the 1770s and 1780s, French chemist Antoine Lavoisier used careful weighing to show that mass is conserved. A burning log seems to vanish, but most of its atoms go into the air as carbon dioxide and water vapour.\n\nChemists write this as equations: 2H2 + O2 → 2H2O. Numbers in front count molecules, and numbers after a symbol count atoms. Because atoms are never created or destroyed, an equation must balance: here, four hydrogen and two oxygen atoms on each side. That lets chemists calculate exactly how much of each ingredient they need."],
    ["Spotting a reaction", "Chemical changes create new substances, and they either release energy or absorb it.",
      "Melting ice or dissolving sugar are physical changes: the substance stays the same, and the change is often reversible. Chemical changes, like rusting or baking a cake, create something new. Clues include a new colour or smell, gas bubbles, light or heat, or a solid forming. Evaporate salt water and you get the salt back, so dissolving is physical. You can't un-bake a cake.\n\nBreaking bonds takes energy; forming bonds releases it. Exothermic reactions release more than they absorb and give off heat, like burning fuel or iron oxidising in hand warmers. Endothermic reactions draw in heat, like photosynthesis storing sunlight in sugar. Even exothermic reactions often need a spark to start, called activation energy."],
    ["Speeding things up", "Heat, concentration, surface area and catalysts all make reactions go faster.",
      "Reactions happen when particles collide with enough energy. Heating makes particles move faster and collide harder. As a rough rule, many reactions go about twice as fast for every 10°C rise, one reason refrigeration slows food spoiling. Higher concentration or pressure means more frequent collisions.\n\nSmaller pieces offer more surface area, which is why fine flour or coal dust can explode while a sack of flour won't. Catalysts speed reactions without being used up, by offering an easier pathway. Your car's catalytic converter turns toxic exhaust gases into less harmful ones, and enzymes are the biological catalysts running your body."],
    ["Oxidation and rust", "Oxidation, losing electrons often to oxygen, drives rusting, burning and the way your cells make energy.",
      "Oxidation once meant combining with oxygen. Now it means losing electrons, and its partner, reduction, means gaining them; the two always happen together.\n\nRusting is slow oxidation of iron into iron oxide, needing oxygen and water. Salt speeds it up, so cars in coastal towns rust faster. Galvanising coats steel in zinc, which corrodes first and protects the iron beneath. Burning is fast oxidation, releasing heat and light. Your cells use controlled oxidation too: cellular respiration combines glucose with oxygen to release energy step by step, producing the carbon dioxide you breathe out."],
    ["Balance and Haber-Bosch", "Reversible reactions settle into equilibrium, and pushing that balance gave us synthetic fertiliser.",
      "Many reactions run both ways. In a closed container they reach equilibrium: forward and reverse reactions happen at the same rate, so amounts stop changing even though molecules keep reacting. In 1884, Henri Le Chatelier described how such a system shifts to partly counteract any disturbance. Haemoglobin picks up oxygen in your lungs, where it is plentiful, and releases it in tissues, where it is scarce.\n\nIn the early 1900s, Fritz Haber and Carl Bosch combined nitrogen from air with hydrogen to make ammonia. Because the reaction is reversible, they used high pressure, high temperature and an iron catalyst to push it towards ammonia quickly enough to be practical. It helps feed around half the world's population, but uses much energy, mostly from natural gas. Haber also developed chemical weapons in World War I: chemistry can serve good or harm."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why must a chemical equation balance?", ["Molecules must always join in pairs","Atoms are never created or destroyed, so counts match both sides","Energy must be identical before and after","Reactions always produce equal masses of gas and solid"], 1, "Reactions only rearrange atoms, so the atom count must match on both sides."],
    [1, "Which of these is a chemical change?", ["Melting ice","Boiling water","Baking a cake","Dissolving sugar in water"], 2, "Baking creates new substances and cannot be reversed, while the others only change form."],
    [2, "Why can fine flour dust explode when a sack of flour won't?", ["Smaller particles expose far more surface area to react","Dust is always hotter than the flour in a sack","Fine flour contains a different chemical from coarse flour","Air pushes sack-sized flour apart"], 0, "More exposed surface means more collisions, so reactions run much faster."],
    [3, "What happens when iron rusts?", ["It dissolves completely in water","It burns quickly and gives off flame","It reacts only with salt and nothing else","It oxidises slowly into iron oxide, using oxygen and water"], 3, "Rusting is slow oxidation; salt only speeds it up."],
    [4, "Why does the Haber-Bosch process use high pressure, heat and a catalyst?", ["To stop the nitrogen from escaping into the air","To push a reversible reaction towards ammonia, quickly","To turn the ammonia into a liquid fuel","To make the reaction safe for workers"], 1, "Controlling the equilibrium and speed made ammonia practical and fertiliser affordable."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A burning log seems to vanish. Where do its atoms actually go?"});

WB.L("acids-bases", "science", "beginner", "Acids and bases",
  "From lemon juice to stomach acid to drain cleaner, acids and bases are everywhere, and the pH scale measures them.",
  [
    ["Sour and slippery", "Acids release hydrogen ions in water, bases accept them, and indicators show which is which.",
      "When acids dissolve in water, they release hydrogen ions (H+). Acids taste sour, like lemons, vinegar and yoghurt, and strong ones corrode metals and burn skin. Bases remove hydrogen ions or release hydroxide ions (OH−); those that dissolve in water are called alkalis. They often feel slippery and taste bitter, like soap. Never taste unknown chemicals: strong bases like drain cleaner are as dangerous as strong acids.\n\nIndicators change colour with acidity. Litmus turns red in acid and blue in base. Red cabbage juice is red-pink in acid, purple near neutral and green or yellow in strong bases. Hydrangeas act as living indicators: blue flowers in acidic soil, pink in alkaline."],
    ["The pH scale", "The pH scale runs from 0 to 14, each step is tenfold, and your body holds blood pH within a narrow range.",
      "pH measures how acidic or basic a solution is, usually from 0 to 14. Pure water is neutral at 7. Lemon juice is about 2, stomach acid about 1.5–3.5, coffee about 5, blood about 7.4, seawater about 8.1 and bleach 12–13. The scale is logarithmic: pH 3 is ten times more acidic than pH 4 and a hundred times more than pH 5. Danish chemist Søren Sørensen introduced it in 1909.\n\nYour stomach makes hydrochloric acid to digest protein and kill microbes, while a layer of mucus stops it digesting the stomach itself. Your blood must stay between about 7.35 and 7.45, as shifts outside that range can be life-threatening. Your lungs and kidneys adjust it constantly, partly through how much carbon dioxide you breathe out."],
    ["Strength and neutralising", "Strong acids split fully into ions; strong is not the same as concentrated, and acids and bases neutralise each other.",
      "Strong and weak don't mean concentrated and dilute. A strong acid, like hydrochloric acid, splits almost completely into ions in water. A weak acid, like the acetic acid in vinegar, releases only a small fraction. Concentration is how much acid is dissolved in the water, so a very dilute strong acid can be gentler than a concentrated weak one. Among bases, sodium hydroxide (some drain cleaners) is strong; ammonia is weak.\n\nAn acid and a base neutralise each other, forming water and a salt. Antacid tablets neutralise excess stomach acid. Farmers spread lime on acidic soils, common in parts of Australia. Baking soda reacts with acidic ingredients, releasing carbon dioxide bubbles that make cakes rise."],
    ["When pH drifts", "Small pH shifts matter: acid dissolves tooth enamel, and the oceans are slowly becoming less alkaline.",
      "Tooth enamel starts to dissolve below about pH 5.5. Frequent sugary drinks, which mouth bacteria turn into acid, and acidic drinks like soft drink and juice can harm teeth. Saliva helps by neutralising acid between meals.\n\nThe oceans absorb roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide humans emit. It forms carbonic acid in seawater, lowering pH. Average surface ocean pH has fallen from about 8.2 to about 8.1 since the industrial revolution. Because the scale is logarithmic, that means roughly 30% more hydrogen ions. The oceans are not acidic yet; they are becoming less alkaline. That makes it harder for corals, oysters and some plankton to build shells and skeletons from calcium carbonate, adding pressure on reefs like the Great Barrier Reef."],
    ["Safe at home", "Many cleaners are strong acids or bases, and some become dangerous when mixed.",
      "Many household cleaners are acids or bases. Descalers and some toilet cleaners are acidic. Oven cleaners, drain cleaners and ammonia sprays are alkaline, as is household bleach, which contains sodium hypochlorite.\n\nNever mix cleaning products. Bleach with acidic cleaners can release chlorine gas, and bleach with ammonia releases toxic chloramine gases; both can badly damage the lungs. Keep chemicals in their original labelled containers, out of reach of children, and wear gloves with strong products. If a chemical is swallowed or splashed in the eyes, rinse with water and call the Poisons Information Centre on 13 11 26, or 000 in an emergency."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which description fits an acid?", ["It removes hydrogen ions and tastes sweet","It never reacts with metals","It releases hydrogen ions when dissolved in water","It always has a pH above 7"], 2, "Acids release H+ ions in water, while bases accept them or release hydroxide ions."],
    [1, "How does a solution at pH 3 compare with one at pH 4?", ["It is ten times more acidic","It is three times more acidic","It is slightly less acidic","It is neutral"], 0, "The pH scale is logarithmic, so each step is a tenfold change."],
    [2, "What makes an acid 'strong' rather than just concentrated?", ["It has a very large amount dissolved in the water","It splits almost completely into ions in water","It tastes more sour than any weak acid","It reacts only with other acids"], 1, "Strength is about how fully an acid ionises; concentration is how much is dissolved."],
    [3, "Why is ocean acidification a concern if the oceans remain slightly alkaline?", ["It makes seawater too acidic to swim in","It turns the ocean a darker colour","It stops the oceans absorbing carbon dioxide","Lower pH hampers corals and oysters building shells"], 3, "Even a small pH drop means about 30% more hydrogen ions, stressing shell-building life."],
    [4, "Why should you never mix bleach with other cleaning products?", ["They can release toxic gases such as chlorine or chloramine","The mixture becomes a strong sweet-smelling base","It makes both cleaners stop working","The container may freeze solid"], 0, "Bleach mixed with acids or ammonia produces gases that can badly damage the lungs."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your blood stays within a hair's breadth of one pH. What holds it there?"});

WB.L("kitchen-chemistry", "food", "beginner", "The chemistry of cooking",
  "Every time you cook, you're running chemistry experiments. Understanding them makes you a better cook.",
  [
    ["Two kinds of browning", "Maillard browning needs proteins and sugars, and caramelisation needs only sugar; both start at high temperatures.",
      "The Maillard reaction creates hundreds of flavour compounds and a brown colour when food with proteins and sugars is heated. It runs quickly above about 140°C, so it happens in a hot pan or oven but not in boiling water, which stays near 100°C. It gives seared steak, toast, roasted coffee and bread crusts their richness. French chemist Louis-Camille Maillard described it in 1912. Pat meat dry and don't crowd the pan: surface water and trapped steam keep it too cool to brown.\n\nCaramelisation needs sugar alone and starts around 160°C for table sugar, producing amber colour and complex, slightly bitter flavour. Fructose caramelises at a lower temperature. Caramel goes from golden to burnt in seconds; starting with a little water heats it more evenly."],
    ["Eggs and starch", "Heat unfolds proteins and swells starch, and that changes the texture of food.",
      "Egg white is mostly water plus folded proteins. Heat makes them unfold, called denaturation, then tangle into a solid network. White begins to set around 62–65°C, and yolk thickens at roughly 65–70°C, which is how chefs make firm whites and runny yolks. Overcook and the network squeezes out water, turning rubbery; the grey-green ring around an overboiled yolk is iron sulfide. Acid can denature proteins too: ceviche 'cooks' fish in citrus juice.\n\nStarch granules absorb water and swell when heated, called gelatinisation, which is how cornflour thickens gravy. On cooling, the chains realign and squeeze out water, called retrogradation. This is the main reason bread goes stale, fastest at fridge temperatures, so keep bread at room temperature or freeze it."],
    ["Fruit and onions", "Cutting cells open lets enzymes meet oxygen or sulfur compounds, which browns fruit and makes you cry.",
      "Slicing an apple, banana or avocado lets an enzyme, polyphenol oxidase, meet oxygen, turning natural polyphenols into brown pigments. It is harmless, and you can slow it. Lemon or lime juice lowers the pH, which the enzyme dislikes, and its vitamin C undoes the first step. Chilling slows it, blanching destroys the enzyme, and pressing cling wrap onto guacamole keeps air out. An avocado stone only protects the flesh it touches.\n\nCutting an onion mixes enzymes with sulfur compounds, making a volatile gas, syn-propanethial-S-oxide, which irritates nerves in your eyes so tears flow. To cry less, chill the onion and use a sharp knife, which crushes fewer cells. Garlic's pungent allicin forms only once a clove is cut or crushed."],
    ["Oil and water", "An emulsifier lets droplets of oil stay suspended in water, as in mayonnaise.",
      "Oil and water don't mix, because water molecules cling to each other and push oil away. An emulsion suspends tiny droplets of one liquid in the other. Mayonnaise is oil droplets in egg yolk and lemon juice or vinegar, held stable by lecithin in the yolk, an emulsifier with one end that attracts water and one that attracts oil.\n\nMilk and butter are natural emulsions too. Vinaigrettes separate because they lack a strong emulsifier; a spoonful of mustard helps hold them together. If mayo 'breaks', slowly whisk it, a little at a time, into a fresh yolk and it can usually be rescued."],
    ["Seasoning logic", "Salt, acid and fat shape flavour, and knowing what each does helps you fix a dish.",
      "Salt does more than make food salty. It suppresses bitterness, enhances other flavours, draws moisture out of vegetables such as eggplant and cabbage, and in brines helps meat hold its juices by changing how proteins behave. Salting pasta water seasons the pasta from within.\n\nAcid, from lemon, vinegar, wine or tomatoes, brightens flavours and balances richness. Fat carries many flavour compounds, which is why low-fat versions can taste flat. Use this to fix dishes: if food tastes flat, it may need salt; if it is heavy or dull, a squeeze of lemon often helps; if it is too sour, a pinch of sugar can balance it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why should you pat meat dry and avoid crowding the pan before searing?", ["Dry meat absorbs more oil from the pan","Surface water and steam keep the meat too cool to brown","Crowding makes the pan heat up unevenly","Water prevents the sugars from forming"], 1, "Maillard browning needs a surface well above 100°C, and moisture holds it down."],
    [1, "What happens to egg white when it is heated?", ["Its proteins unfold and tangle into a solid network","Its water boils away entirely","Its sugars caramelise into a solid","Its fats melt and set"], 0, "Heat denatures the proteins, turning clear liquid into firm, opaque white."],
    [2, "Why does lemon juice slow browning in cut fruit?", ["It seals the surface from the air with an oily film","It destroys the enzyme completely","It dries out the surface of the fruit","Its acid and vitamin C interfere with the enzyme"], 3, "Acid and vitamin C interfere with the enzyme chemistry that turns polyphenols brown."],
    [3, "Why does mayonnaise hold together when a plain vinaigrette separates?", ["Oil mixes fully with water when it is whisked hard","Lecithin in egg yolk acts as an emulsifier","Vinegar is heavier than lemon juice","Cold oil is thicker than warm oil"], 1, "An emulsifier has an end attracting water and an end attracting oil, keeping droplets suspended."],
    [4, "A dish tastes heavy and dull. What is a sensible fix?", ["Add a squeeze of lemon, since acid brightens food","Add more fat to carry the flavour","Cook it longer to concentrate it","Add a pinch of baking soda"], 0, "Acid balances richness, while a lack of salt makes food taste flat."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why does a seared steak taste richer than a boiled one? It's chemistry."});

WB.L("newtons-laws", "science", "beginner", "Newton's laws of motion",
  "Three simple rules explain why seatbelts save lives, rockets fly, and a heavy trolley is hard to stop.",
  [
    ["Newton and inertia", "Objects keep doing what they are doing unless an outside force acts on them.",
      "Newton's great book began with a question. In 1684, Edmond Halley asked what path a planet follows if the Sun's pull weakens with the square of the distance. Newton answered at once: an ellipse. The work grew into the Principia, published in 1687 and paid for by Halley. It set out three laws still used to design cars, bridges, rollercoasters and spacecraft.\n\nThe first law says an object at rest stays at rest, and a moving object keeps moving in a straight line at the same speed, unless an outside force acts on it. The more mass, the more inertia. Friction hides this on Earth, so for centuries people believed motion needs a constant push. In a crash, the car stops but your body keeps moving forward: that is why seatbelts and airbags save lives."],
    ["Force and acceleration", "Force equals mass times acceleration, so more force or less mass means more acceleration.",
      "The second law says force equals mass times acceleration: F = ma. Push an empty shopping trolley and it speeds up easily; push a full one with the same force and it barely moves. A loaded truck needs far more braking force than a car. Force is measured in newtons: one newton accelerates one kilogram by one metre per second, every second, roughly the weight of a small apple.\n\nAcceleration happens only when forces are unbalanced. Friction resists surfaces sliding and is essential for tyres to grip and for you to walk. Air resistance, or drag, rises rapidly with speed, so driving faster uses more fuel. A skydiver speeds up until drag balances their weight, reaching about 200 km/h belly-down."],
    ["Action and reaction", "For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction, acting on different objects.",
      "When you push on something, it pushes back just as hard in the opposite direction. When you jump, you push down on the Earth and it pushes you up. The Earth moves too, but immeasurably little because its mass is so huge.\n\nA rocket pushes exhaust gases backwards at high speed, and they push the rocket forwards. It needs no air to push against, which is why rockets work in space. The two forces act on different objects, so they don't cancel out. The recoil of a gun and the kick of a fire hose are the same law at work."],
    ["Momentum and force", "Momentum is mass times velocity, and spreading a change in momentum over more time reduces the force.",
      "Momentum is mass times velocity, so a slow truck can carry as much as a fast, much lighter car. In a collision the total momentum stays the same, merely shared differently. That is why Newton's cradle keeps clicking, and a cue ball can stop dead while the ball it hits rolls on.\n\nChanging momentum takes a force applied over time, so stretching out the time reduces the force. Crumple zones lengthen a collision, lowering the force on passengers. Bending your knees when landing from a jump, or a cricketer drawing their hands back to catch a ball, does the same."],
    ["Circles and everywhere", "Circular motion needs a force towards the centre, and the same few laws explain sport, travel and space.",
      "Moving objects travel in straight lines, so anything moving in a circle needs a constant pull towards the centre: centripetal force. For a car on a corner it is tyre friction; for the Moon, Earth's gravity; for washing in a spinning machine, the drum wall. The outward 'fling' you feel is not a real force. It is your inertia trying to carry you straight on.\n\nA footballer's kick accelerates the ball (second law). A swimmer pushes water back to move forward (third law). Engineers use the laws for safer car seats and helmets, and NASA still uses them for most spacecraft trajectories. Newton said he saw further by standing on the shoulders of giants, among them Galileo and Kepler."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why do seatbelts save lives in a crash?", ["They stop the car from rolling over","They push you backwards away from the dashboard","The car stops suddenly but your body keeps moving forward unless restrained","They spread the car's momentum evenly"], 2, "Inertia keeps you moving, so the belt supplies the force that stops you with the car."],
    [1, "You push a full trolley and an empty one with the same force. What happens?", ["The full one accelerates less, as it has more mass","Both accelerate equally because the force is the same","The full one accelerates more because it has more inertia","Neither moves unless the force doubles"], 0, "F = ma means that, for the same force, more mass gives less acceleration."],
    [2, "Why can rockets work in space where there is no air?", ["They push against the thin gas that fills space","They use the Sun's pull to move them along","Their engines work only while they are in the atmosphere","They push exhaust gases backwards, and the gases push the rocket forwards"], 3, "Action and reaction need no medium; the exhaust and the rocket push on each other."],
    [3, "How do crumple zones reduce injuries in a crash?", ["They stop the car's momentum from changing at all","They lengthen the collision time, lowering the force","They make the car heavier so it stops sooner","They absorb the crash by removing the car's mass"], 1, "A given change in momentum over more time needs less force."],
    [4, "What is the outward 'fling' you feel when a car turns a corner?", ["Your inertia trying to carry you straight on","A real force pushing you towards the outside","Centripetal force pulling you outwards","Friction between you and the seat"], 0, "The only real force is the inward one; your body simply resists changing direction."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why a loaded truck is so hard to stop, and what a seatbelt really does."});

WB.L("gravity", "science", "beginner", "Gravity",
  "It holds you to the ground, keeps the Moon in orbit and shapes galaxies. It's also the weakest force in nature.",
  [
    ["One universal force", "Every mass attracts every other mass, with a pull that grows with mass and falls with distance squared.",
      "Newton realised the force pulling an apple down is the same one holding the Moon in orbit. His law of universal gravitation, published in 1687, says every object attracts every other, with a force that increases with their masses and decreases with the square of the distance between them. Double the distance and gravity drops to a quarter; triple it and it drops to a ninth.\n\nIn 1798, Henry Cavendish measured the tiny pull between lead balls in his laboratory, which allowed Earth's density and mass to be calculated. You are gravitationally attracting the device in your hand right now, just very, very weakly."],
    ["Mass, weight and falling", "Objects fall at the same rate without air resistance, and weight changes with gravity while mass does not.",
      "Aristotle taught that heavy objects fall faster, and for almost 2,000 years few checked. Around 1600 Galileo showed otherwise, most likely by rolling balls down ramps rather than, as legend says, dropping them from the Leaning Tower of Pisa. Heavier objects feel a stronger pull but also resist acceleration more, so the effects cancel exactly. In 1971, Apollo 15 astronaut David Scott dropped a hammer and a falcon feather on the airless Moon, and they landed together.\n\nYour mass, how much matter you contain, is the same everywhere. Your weight, the force of gravity on that mass, is not. On the Moon, with about one-sixth of Earth's gravity, a 70 kg person would read about 12 kg on Earth scales; on Mars about 26 kg. Near Earth's surface, falling objects speed up by about 9.8 m/s every second."],
    ["Orbits and tides", "Orbiting is falling around a planet, and gravity's change with distance stretches whole moons.",
      "Newton imagined firing cannonballs from a mountaintop faster and faster, until one curved down exactly as fast as Earth's surface curved away. It would fall forever without landing: an orbit. The International Space Station, about 400 km up at roughly 28,000 km/h, circles Earth about every 90 minutes. Astronauts float not because gravity is absent, since it is about 90% as strong there, but because they and the station fall together. Geostationary satellites orbit about 36,000 km up, keeping pace with Earth's rotation.\n\nBecause gravity weakens with distance, a planet pulls harder on a moon's near side than its far side. Jupiter's tides melt the interior of Io, the most volcanically active world in the solar system. In 1992 Jupiter tore Comet Shoemaker-Levy 9 into more than 20 pieces, which hit the planet in 1994."],
    ["Weak but winning", "Gravity is the weakest force, yet it always attracts and adds up, and Einstein described it as curved spacetime.",
      "Gravity is by far the weakest of nature's four fundamental forces. A small fridge magnet holds itself up against the pull of the entire Earth. Between two protons, electric repulsion is roughly 10^36 times stronger. Yet gravity rules the cosmos because electric charges, positive and negative, mostly cancel in bulk matter, while gravity only attracts and keeps adding up across unlimited distances.\n\nNewton admitted he couldn't explain how gravity acts across empty space. In 1915, Einstein's general relativity said massive objects curve space and time, and other objects follow those curves. So gravity isn't instant: if the Sun vanished, Earth would circle its empty position for about eight minutes. In 2017, gravitational waves and light from a neutron star collision arrived within two seconds of each other after 130 million years, confirming gravity travels at light speed."],
    ["Gravity and your body", "Gravity constantly loads your bones, muscles and spine, and it varies slightly across Earth.",
      "In orbit, without the constant load of weight, astronauts can lose around 1% of bone density in weight-bearing bones each month. They exercise about two hours a day to limit muscle and bone loss. Their spines lengthen, making them a few centimetres taller, and fluids shift towards the head, giving a puffy face.\n\nOn Earth, gravity squeezes your spinal discs through the day, so you are slightly taller in the morning. Gravity isn't uniform either: because of Earth's spin and bulging equator, you weigh about 0.5% less at the equator than at the poles."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "If you double the distance between two masses, what happens to the gravitational pull?", ["It drops to a half","It drops to a quarter","It stays the same","It drops to a tenth"], 1, "Gravity falls with the square of distance, so double the distance gives a quarter of the force."],
    [1, "Why do a hammer and a feather fall together on the Moon?", ["A heavier object's extra pull is cancelled by its extra inertia","The Moon's gravity affects feathers more strongly","Heavier objects are always pulled less by gravity","The hammer is slowed by lunar dust"], 0, "Pull and resistance to acceleration both scale with mass, so all objects fall at the same rate."],
    [2, "Why do astronauts float aboard the International Space Station?", ["Gravity is almost absent at that height","The station spins fast enough to cancel gravity","They and the station are in continuous free fall","Space itself pushes objects upwards"], 2, "Gravity there is about 90% as strong; orbiting is continuous falling."],
    [3, "Why does gravity dominate the cosmos despite being the weakest force?", ["It is stronger than electricity at very short range","It acts instantly, unlike the other forces","Its strength grows as objects move apart","It only attracts, so its effects add up across huge masses and distances"], 3, "Electric charges cancel in bulk matter, but gravity always adds."],
    [4, "Why do astronauts lose bone density in orbit?", ["Space radiation dissolves the minerals in bone","Their bones lack the constant load of weight","They breathe less oxygen than on Earth","Their bodies stop making blood"], 1, "Bones respond to load, which is why astronauts exercise about two hours a day."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Gravity is the weakest force in nature. So why does it shape galaxies?"});

WB.L("energy", "science", "beginner", "Energy: what it is and where it goes",
  "Energy can't be created or destroyed, only changed from one form to another. Almost all of it on Earth started in the Sun.",
  [
    ["Changed, never lost", "Energy is the ability to do work, and it changes form without ever being created or destroyed.",
      "Energy is what makes things happen. Physicists define it as the capacity to do work, applying a force over a distance. Kinetic energy is the energy of motion. Potential energy is stored, like water behind a dam or a stretched rubber band. There are also chemical, thermal, electrical, light, sound and nuclear forms. Energy is measured in joules, named after English brewer and physicist James Joule; Australian food labels use kilojoules.\n\nEnergy is never created or destroyed. In the 1840s, Joule showed that a set amount of mechanical work always produces the same amount of heat. Riding downhill turns potential into kinetic energy; braking turns it into heat in the pads. When we 'waste' energy, we convert it into forms less useful to us."],
    ["Speed squares energy", "Kinetic energy rises with the square of speed, so doubling speed quadruples it.",
      "Kinetic energy equals half the mass times the speed squared. Double your speed and your kinetic energy quadruples. A car at 60 km/h carries four times the energy of the same car at 30 km/h. The brakes, or in a crash the vehicle and the people inside, must absorb all of it.\n\nThat is why braking distances grow so quickly with speed, and why lower limits in busy areas, like 40 km/h school zones, save lives. Small increases in speed matter more than they feel."],
    ["Nothing is 100% efficient", "Every conversion wastes some energy as heat, which spreads out and becomes less useful.",
      "Old incandescent bulbs turned only about 5% of electricity into light; LEDs use roughly 75–80% less electricity for the same light. Petrol engines turn roughly 20–30% of fuel energy into motion, while electric motors manage around 90%. Muscles convert only about a quarter of the energy they use into movement, the rest becoming heat, which is why exercise warms you up.\n\nHeat pumps, including reverse-cycle air conditioners, seem to beat the rules, but they move heat rather than make it, delivering three or more units of heat per unit of electricity. The underlying principle is the second law of thermodynamics: energy tends to spread out and become less concentrated, which is why perpetual motion machines are impossible."],
    ["The Sun's power", "Most energy on Earth comes from the Sun, which gets its own from converting mass.",
      "Plants capture sunlight through photosynthesis, feeding almost every food chain. Coal, oil and gas are the remains of ancient plants and marine organisms, so they are stored ancient sunlight. Wind comes from the Sun heating Earth unevenly; hydro depends on rain, which depends on the Sun evaporating water. Sunlight striking Earth in an hour or two carries about as much energy as humanity uses in a year. The exceptions are nuclear energy, geothermal heat and tidal energy, which comes mainly from the Moon's gravity.\n\nThe Sun runs on E = mc², Einstein's 1905 finding that mass is a form of energy. Fusion converts about four million tonnes of the Sun's mass into energy every second. One gram of matter, fully converted, would release roughly the energy of 20,000 tonnes of TNT."],
    ["Power and your body", "Power is the rate of using energy, in watts, and your body runs on food energy in kilojoules.",
      "Power is how fast energy is transferred: one watt is one joule per second. A kettle might draw 2,000 watts or more, a laptop about 50 and a phone charger 5–20. Electricity bills charge in kilowatt-hours: 1,000 watts for an hour, like running a small heater. The biggest home users are heating and cooling, hot water, clothes dryers and pool pumps, and standby power adds up. Energy Rating Label stars and full dishwasher loads help cut bills.\n\nYour body is an energy converter too. One food calorie is about 4.2 kilojoules, and Australian labels use 8,700 kilojoules a day as a reference for an average adult, though needs vary. Even at rest, your brain uses about a fifth of your energy."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the law of conservation of energy say?", ["Energy is gradually used up as machines run","Moving objects create new energy as they speed up","Heat is a form that cannot change into anything else","Energy changes form, but the total stays the same"], 3, "When we say energy is 'wasted', it has simply become a less useful form such as heat."],
    [1, "What happens to a car's kinetic energy if its speed doubles?", ["It quadruples","It doubles","It stays the same","It increases by half"], 0, "Kinetic energy depends on speed squared, which is why small speed increases matter."],
    [2, "Why do heat pumps seem to beat the efficiency rules?", ["They create extra energy from the air","They move existing heat instead of making it","They run on a hidden second power source","They store heat from the previous day"], 1, "Delivering three or more units of heat per unit of electricity doesn't break conservation, because the heat is moved."],
    [3, "Why are coal, oil and gas described as stored sunlight?", ["They were formed by sunlight heating rocks directly","They are produced by sunlight each year","They are the remains of ancient plants and organisms that captured the Sun's energy","They absorb sunlight from the air as they burn"], 2, "Photosynthesis stored sunlight as chemical energy, then the remains were buried for millions of years."],
    [4, "What does one kilowatt-hour measure?", ["Using 1,000 watts for one hour","The power of any appliance at one moment","Using 1,000 watts for one second","The energy in 1,000 kilojoules of food"], 0, "Bills charge for energy over time, so power multiplied by hours matters."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Double your speed and you quadruple your energy. Here's why it matters."});

WB.L("electricity", "science", "beginner", "How electricity works",
  "Flick a switch and the lights come on. Behind that is a flow of electrons, a power station, and a grid spanning a continent.",
  [
    ["Charge and current", "Current is the flow of charge; individual electrons move slowly, but the push travels very fast.",
      "In metals like copper, some outer electrons are free to wander between atoms. When a battery pushes them, they drift through the wire as an electric current. Individual electrons move very slowly, often less than a millimetre per second, but the push travels at a large fraction of light speed, like pushing water into a full pipe. Copper is used for most wiring because it conducts well and costs far less than silver, the best conductor.\n\nStatic electricity is charge that builds up rather than flows. Rub a balloon on your hair and electrons move to the balloon, so it clings to walls. Lightning is the same on a colossal scale: ice particles colliding in storm clouds separate charge until a discharge leaps to the ground, heating the air to around 30,000 degrees. Thunder is that explosive expansion."],
    ["Volts, amps and loops", "Voltage pushes, current flows and resistance opposes, and current needs a complete circuit.",
      "Voltage, in volts, is the electrical pressure pushing charge around a circuit. Current, in amps, is the charge flowing per second. Resistance, in ohms, is how strongly a material opposes flow. Georg Ohm's 1827 law says voltage equals current times resistance. Voltage times current gives power in watts. Australian household supply is 230 volts, against about 120 in North America.\n\nCurrent flows only around a complete loop, and a switch breaks or connects it. In a series circuit one failure stops everything, like some old Christmas lights. In a parallel circuit each component has its own path, as in house wiring, so one broken appliance doesn't cut power to the rest."],
    ["Turbine to home", "Most electricity comes from spinning magnets near coils, and AC lets us send it over long distances.",
      "In 1831, Michael Faraday found that moving a magnet near a coil of wire produces a current. Almost all power stations use this: something spins a turbine connected to a generator. Coal and nuclear plants boil water for steam, gas plants mostly use hot combustion gases, and wind and hydro spin turbines directly. Solar panels turn light directly into electricity in silicon, with no moving parts.\n\nGrids use alternating current, which reverses 50 times a second in Australia. In the 1880s, Edison championed direct current and Tesla and Westinghouse championed AC. AC won because transformers raise its voltage for long-distance transmission, then lower it for homes. High voltage means lower current, and less energy wasted as heat in the wires. Some very long or undersea cables, like Basslink, use high-voltage DC."],
    ["The national grid", "Supply must match demand every second, and batteries and rooftop solar are changing the grid.",
      "Australia's main grid, the National Electricity Market, links Queensland, New South Wales, the ACT, Victoria, South Australia and Tasmania. It stretches around 5,000 km, one of the longest interconnected systems in the world. Western Australia and the Northern Territory run separate systems.\n\nBecause electricity is hard to store at scale, supply must match demand every moment, and operators balance it around the clock. Big batteries, pumped hydro schemes like Snowy 2.0, and rooftop solar are changing how the grid works. In South Australia, rooftop solar has at times supplied most of the state's demand, while evenings, when solar fades and households cook and run heaters, remain the peak."],
    ["Electrical safety", "Current through the body is what harms, so water, damaged cords and overloaded boards are key hazards.",
      "It is the current through the body that makes shocks dangerous, not voltage alone, and wet skin has much lower resistance, so far more current flows. Keep appliances away from baths, sinks and pools. Never use damaged cords, and don't overload power boards or plug them into each other.\n\nFuses and circuit breakers cut power when too much current flows. Safety switches (RCDs) detect current leaking to earth, perhaps through a person, and cut power in a fraction of a second. Test them with their test button regularly; many authorities suggest every three months. Fixed wiring in Australia must be done by a licensed electrician. If someone is being electrocuted, don't touch them: switch off the power if it's safe, then call 000. Stay at least eight metres from fallen power lines and treat them as live."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Electrons drift slowly, so why does a light come on instantly?", ["Electrons speed up to light speed once the switch closes","The push travels through the wire at a large fraction of light speed","The light stores energy and releases it on demand","Electrons are already waiting inside the bulb"], 1, "Like pushing water into a full pipe, the effect moves far faster than each electron."],
    [1, "Why does house wiring use parallel circuits?", ["Each appliance has its own path, so one failure doesn't cut power to the rest","They make the voltage higher for each appliance","They need less copper than series wiring","They let current flow in both directions at once"], 0, "In series, one broken component breaks the loop for everything."],
    [2, "Why is electricity sent over long distances at high voltage?", ["High voltage makes the electricity travel faster than light","Transformers can only work at high voltage","High voltage turns alternating current into direct current","Higher voltage means lower current, which wastes less energy as heat in the wires"], 3, "Transformers make AC practical for transmission, then lower the voltage for homes."],
    [3, "Why must electricity supply match demand every moment?", ["Generators cannot be turned off once started","Power lines hold only a few seconds of electricity","Electricity is hard to store at scale","Homes use exactly the same amount all day"], 2, "Big batteries and pumped hydro are growing, but operators still balance the grid around the clock."],
    [4, "What does a safety switch (RCD) do?", ["Limits the voltage in your home to a safe level","Detects leaking current and cuts power in an instant","Stops lightning from reaching your wiring","Turns off power only when a wire overheats"], 1, "RCDs cut the power in a fraction of a second, which is why you should test them regularly."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Electrons crawl through a wire, yet the light comes on instantly. How?"});

WB.L("light", "science", "beginner", "Light and colour",
  "Light is the fastest thing in the universe, it's both a wave and a particle, and colour doesn't exist without a brain to see it.",
  [
    ["The cosmic speed limit", "Light travels at about 300,000 kilometres per second, and nothing with mass can match that speed.",
      "Light moves at 299,792 kilometres per second in a vacuum, usually rounded to 300,000. In one second it could circle the Earth about seven and a half times. Moonlight takes about 1.3 seconds to reach us, sunlight about 8 minutes and 20 seconds, and light from Proxima Centauri, the nearest star beyond the Sun, over four years.\n\nSo when you look at distant stars, you're looking into the past: their light left years, centuries or millennia ago. Light slows down in glass and water, but nothing with mass can ever reach its speed in a vacuum."],
    ["The hidden spectrum", "Visible light is a narrow band of the electromagnetic spectrum, which runs from radio waves to gamma rays.",
      "Light is electromagnetic radiation, a travelling wave of electric and magnetic fields, as Scottish physicist James Clerk Maxwell showed in the 1860s. Visible light is only a narrow band, with wavelengths of about 400 to 700 nanometres.\n\nLonger wavelengths include infrared, which you feel as heat, then microwaves and radio waves. Shorter ones include ultraviolet, which causes sunburn, then X-rays and gamma rays. They're all the same kind of thing, differing only in wavelength and energy.\n\nSome animals sense beyond our range. Bees see ultraviolet patterns on flowers that are invisible to us, and some snakes detect the infrared glow of warm-blooded prey."],
    ["Bending light apart", "Light bends when it changes material, and different colours bend by different amounts, which splits white light into a rainbow.",
      "Smooth surfaces such as mirrors and still water reflect light at the same angle it arrives, which is why you see clear images in them. When light passes from one material into another, such as air into water, it changes speed and bends. This is refraction. It makes a straw look broken in a glass and a pool look shallower than it is. Lenses in glasses, cameras and eyes use it to focus light, and optical fibres use total internal reflection to carry internet data across oceans.\n\nIn the 1660s, Isaac Newton split sunlight with a prism, then recombined it, showing that white light is a mixture. Violet bends most and red least. Raindrops do the same job: light enters, reflects off the back and bends again on the way out. You see a rainbow with the Sun behind you."],
    ["Why things have colour", "Objects look coloured because they absorb some wavelengths and reflect others, and your brain turns the signals into colour.",
      "A banana looks yellow because it reflects mostly yellowish wavelengths and absorbs the rest. A black T-shirt absorbs most light, so it heats up in the sun. The sky is blue because air molecules scatter short blue wavelengths far more than red, a process called Rayleigh scattering. At sunrise and sunset light crosses far more air, so blue is scattered away and reds and oranges remain. Clouds look white because their larger droplets scatter all colours equally.\n\nColour itself is made by your brain. Your eyes have three types of cone cell, and the brain compares their signals. That's why screens make almost any colour from red, green and blue dots, and why magenta, which matches no single wavelength, still looks real. About 8% of men and 0.5% of women of Northern European descent have some colour vision deficiency."],
    ["Wave, particle, laser", "Light behaves as both a wave and a stream of particles called photons, and lasers put its waves in step.",
      "In the early 1800s, Thomas Young shone light through two narrow slits and saw bands of light and dark, as overlapping water waves would make. That was strong evidence for a wave. But in 1905 Einstein explained the photoelectric effect, where light knocks electrons out of metal, by treating light as particles, later called photons. He won the 1921 Nobel Prize for this, not for relativity. Light acts as both, depending on how it's measured, a puzzle at the heart of quantum physics.\n\nA laser produces waves of one wavelength, marching in step, in a narrow beam. Einstein predicted the process in 1917 and Theodore Maiman built the first working laser in 1960. Lasers now read barcodes, carry data, cut steel and reshape corneas. Powerful pointers can damage eyes and are restricted in Australia."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does it mean to look at a distant star?", ["You see light that left it years, centuries or millennia ago","You see the star exactly as it is right now","You see light that has slowed to a crawl in space","You see heat that has not yet turned into light"], 0, "Light takes time to travel, so distant stars are seen as they were in the past."],
    [1, "How does visible light relate to infrared and ultraviolet?", ["They are unrelated kinds of energy with different sources","Infrared and ultraviolet are particles, while light is a wave","They exist only in space, never on Earth's surface","All are the same kind of wave, differing in wavelength and energy"], 3, "The whole electromagnetic spectrum is one kind of wave, and visible light is a narrow band of it."],
    [2, "What did Newton show by splitting sunlight with a prism?", ["Prisms add colour to light that was colourless","Violet light travels faster than red light in space","White light is a mixture of colours that can be recombined","Sunlight is mostly invisible infrared radiation"], 2, "Colours bend by different amounts, so a prism separates white light and a second prism recombines it."],
    [3, "Why is the sky blue?", ["The sky reflects the colour of the oceans below","Air molecules scatter short blue wavelengths more than red ones","Blue is the true colour of sunlight at midday","Dust in the air absorbs every colour except blue"], 1, "This is Rayleigh scattering, and at sunset the blue is scattered out of the longer light path."],
    [4, "What does light's behaviour in different experiments suggest?", ["It acts as a wave or as particles, depending on how it is measured","It is purely a wave and never behaves as particles","It is purely a stream of particles, never a wave","It switches nature depending on who is observing it"], 0, "Interference patterns show wave behaviour, while the photoelectric effect shows particle behaviour."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Colour isn't out in the world at all. So where does it come from?"});

WB.L("relativity", "science", "advanced", "Einstein's relativity, simply",
  "Moving clocks run slow, space bends, and your head ages faster than your feet. Relativity is strange, and it's been confirmed again and again.",
  [
    ["Light's fixed speed", "The speed of light is the same for every observer, however they are moving, and that single fact upends our ideas of time.",
      "If you throw a ball forward at 20 km/h on a train doing 100 km/h, someone on the platform sees it move at 120 km/h. Light doesn't work that way. In 1887, Michelson and Morley tried to detect Earth's motion through a supposed 'ether' that carried light waves. They found nothing: light's speed came out the same in every direction.\n\nLight always travels at about 300,000 km per second, whether you move towards it or away. In 1905, Albert Einstein, then a 26-year-old clerk at the Swiss patent office in Bern, took this as a basic law and followed it to astonishing conclusions."],
    ["Slow clocks, short rulers", "Moving clocks run slow and moving objects shorten along their direction of travel, which is why nothing with mass can reach light speed.",
      "If light's speed is fixed for everyone, time must give. A clock moving relative to you runs slower from your point of view: time dilation. At everyday speeds the effect is tiny but measurable. In 1971, atomic clocks flown around the world on airliners differed from ground clocks by billionths of a second, as predicted. Muons, created when cosmic rays hit the upper atmosphere, should mostly decay before reaching the ground, yet many arrive, because their clocks run slow.\n\nFast-moving objects also contract along their direction of motion, though travellers notice nothing. Near light speed, each extra push takes more and more energy, and reaching it would take infinite energy. The Large Hadron Collider pushes protons beyond 99.999999% of light speed, but never to it."],
    ["The twin puzzle", "A twin who travels near light speed and returns is younger than the twin who stayed home, because only the traveller changes reference frames.",
      "Imagine one twin staying on Earth while the other flies to a distant star and back at close to light speed. On return, the traveller is younger, perhaps by years. It seems paradoxical, since each twin sees the other moving. But the situations aren't symmetrical. The traveller accelerates, turns around and comes back, changing reference frames. The Earth twin doesn't.\n\nThe effect is real on small scales. Astronauts on the International Space Station, moving at about 28,000 km/h, age a few thousandths of a second less than people on Earth over a six-month mission."],
    ["Spacetime and gravity", "Space and time form one fabric called spacetime, and in general relativity mass curves it, which we feel as gravity.",
      "In 1908, Einstein's former teacher Hermann Minkowski showed that relativity treats space and time as one four-dimensional fabric: spacetime. Observers in motion slice it into 'space' and 'time' differently, so they can disagree about whether two distant events were simultaneous, and neither is wrong. There is no universal 'now'.\n\nGeneral relativity, completed in 1915, covered gravity. Einstein realised that free fall feels exactly like floating without gravity, which he called the happiest thought of his life. Mass and energy curve spacetime, and objects follow the curves. As John Wheeler put it, spacetime tells matter how to move; matter tells spacetime how to curve. The theory explained a drift in Mercury's orbit and predicted that light bends around massive objects. Arthur Eddington's 1919 eclipse expeditions confirmed it, making Einstein famous overnight."],
    ["Cosmic lenses, pocket proof", "Curved spacetime bends light and slows clocks, and GPS satellites must correct for both effects every day.",
      "A massive galaxy cluster can act as a giant lens, bending and magnifying the light of objects far behind it. Einstein worked this out in 1936 but thought it would never be seen. The first lensed quasar was found in 1979, and the James Webb telescope now uses lensing to study galaxies too faint to see otherwise. A perfect alignment smears the background object into an Einstein ring. Relativity also predicts that a spinning mass drags spacetime around with it, which NASA's Gravity Probe B measured around Earth, reporting in 2011.\n\nGravity slows time too: clocks run faster where gravity is weaker, so your head ages slightly faster than your feet. GPS satellites gain a net 38 microseconds a day from both effects. Uncorrected, positions would drift by about 10 kilometres daily."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was so strange about the speed of light?", ["It depends on how fast the light source is moving","It is the same for every observer, however they are moving","It increases when you move towards the light","It changes with the direction of Earth's travel"], 1, "Michelson and Morley found no difference in any direction, and Einstein took constant light speed as a law."],
    [1, "Why can't anything with mass reach the speed of light?", ["Reaching it would take infinite energy, as each extra bit of speed takes more","Air friction slows every object before it gets there","Gravity pulls fast objects back towards the Earth","Fast objects shrink until they vanish from view"], 0, "Accelerators get protons extremely close to light speed, but extra energy mostly adds energy rather than speed."],
    [2, "Why does the travelling twin return younger?", ["Space travel slows the ageing of every living thing","Each twin sees the other ageing faster, so they cancel out","The Earth twin ages faster because gravity is stronger","Only the traveller accelerates, turns and changes reference frames"], 3, "The twins' situations are not symmetrical, so the traveller returns younger."],
    [3, "In general relativity, what is gravity?", ["A force carried between objects by an invisible ether","A property found only in very large objects","The effect of mass and energy curving spacetime","Light pressure pushing objects towards each other"], 2, "Objects follow curves in spacetime, and starlight bending past the Sun in 1919 supported the idea."],
    [4, "Why do GPS satellites need relativity corrections?", ["Their orbits are too elliptical to calculate","Speed and weaker gravity change their clock rates, causing drift of kilometres a day","Their radio signals are slowed down by the atmosphere","Phones cannot read signals from fast-moving objects"], 1, "Without correction, positions would drift by about 10 kilometres a day."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your head ages faster than your feet. Here's why your phone map relies on it."});

